warframe has grown from a modest  free-to-play experiment that was very  much a work in progress to an impressive  online game with an ever-growing fan  base since its launch in 2013 the action  RPG starring space ninjas slashing and  shooting across the universe has evolved  in a big way after its many expansions  giving players more ways to customize  their characters and team up in  different scenarios to take on the  toughest challenges in the game not long  after the release of fortuna the largest  expansion to date the developers of  digital Xtreme and along with switchport  veterans panic button games will launch  their most ambitious project to date a  port of the massive game to the Nintendo  switch we recently tried out the newest  version of warframe prior to its launch  and came away pleased with how faithful  it was to the previous versions of the  game  set for release on November 20th  warframe brings its fast-paced and  kinetic game played in Nintendo's  platform marking the first time the game  will be playable in portable format  diving into warfare him for the first  time can be daunting but once you get  into the swing of it you'll find a game  that offers a lot of variety and systems  to dive into showing similar shades to  the power grind and gameplay found in  destiny as one of the switches few  online only games putting in the same  company as the immensely popular  fortnight warframe is a games as a  service title with its own unique online  infrastructure and won't require players  to have access to in a Nintendo online  at launch the switch version will offer  14 different planets to travel to over  200 different melee weapons and firearms  to collect more than 30 unique  characters to level up and master and a  detailed plot showing the escalating war  among the different factions vying for  control in collaboration with panic  button games the same studio behind the  impressive switch ports for doom 2016  and Wolfenstein 2 the new Colossus the  two developers worked extensively to  make sure that the next port of the game  could stand up alongside the others if  you're familiar with the feel and play  style the ps4 and Xbox one editions of  warframe then you'll be able to pick up  the game quite easily  however some clear concessions were made  to ensure that the game was in stable  shape for the portable hardware running  out of mostly stable 30 frames per  second and at 720p and both docked and  undocked States warframe on switch will  likely take some adjusting to four  players who've invested many hours in  other versions which were uncomfortably  at 60fps moreover there's some  occasional dips in framerate during the  more intense encounters particularly  when multiple players use their powers  and weapons simultaneously while the  developers acknowledge the technical  changes between the different releases  they stated that the primary focus was  to keep the action smooth and the  framerate stable  for the most part the switch version  does a solid job of keeping up with the  gameplay while also highlighting the  strengths of the console swapping  between docked and undocked modes is  simple and painless and there isn't much  difference in performance between the  two which is great when you want to  bundle up with the game in handheld mode  after playing in docked the switch  version also features motion control  functionality letting you perform some  actions with the motion of your wrist  such as fishing shooting your firearms  and using other tools in game the switch  release also features integrated voice  chat allowing party members to  communicate with each other without the  need of the Nintendo app if you're a  seasoned player then you can also import  your account from the PC over to the  switch unfortunately the switch version  will not offer cross play opting to  stick with its own player base in  ecosystem when you really look at it  there's really no other game on the  switch like warframe though it's been  often seen as the destiny before destiny  it certainly goes out of its way to  offer up options and different  adventures for players to dive into it's  one of those games that's gotten better  with age and with the amount of content  that's still on the way warfare months  which will be a solid entry point for  those looking for a sizable game to dive  into which has kept its unique sense of  style and flow very much attacked on the  new platform
 

